WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.671
I’m sure you all have had this discussion
with your friends at

00:00:10.671 --> 00:00:11.671
some point…

00:00:11.671 --> 00:00:13.670
Why are flat rents getting so expensive?

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.230
It
doesn’t matter if you live in Los Angeles,

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:20.349
San Francisco or
London: we are talking about a global problem.

00:00:20.349 --> 00:00:23.040
Then again,
we should first question… is this an actual

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:30.320
problem or is it
just normal economics?

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.570
Whatever it is, we’re talking about a phenomenon
that’s

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.640
happening in all the big metropolises.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.220
For example, in the
city of Los Angeles, the average rent has

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:38.890
risen 15% in the
last 3 years.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.780
And let’s be honest here… if you have
a

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.540
decent salary, this is more of an inconvenience
than a real

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.540
problem.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.989
But let’s imagine that you’re a shop assistant
in L.A. Even

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:52.480
better, let’s imagine that ERIK is a shop
assistant in L.A.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:53.480
What would happen to you?

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.480
Even better, let’s imagine that XANDER is
a shop assistant

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.480
in L.A. What would happen to you?

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.699
If I were a shop assistant in L.A. I would
probably be

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:00.070
making around 2000 USD a month.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.890
A 540 square foot
single room apartment in South Los Angeles,

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:07.540
which is a
very cheap neighbourhood, can cost 1200 USD

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.630
a month.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:14.860
That’s 60% of my salary.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.640
The rest would go to basic needs
like food and clothing.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.860
And yes, I would have a hard time
saving any money in that situation.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.530
In an ideal situation, you would only spend
a third of your

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.360
salary on your rent or your mortgage, so you’d
have enough

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.330
to live comfortably and to save.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.460
Spending 60% of your
income on the rent means that just a small

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.200
increase in
prices could knock you below the poverty line.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:47.330
And yes, that
risk is more real than we might want to believe.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:51.000
Los Angeles homeless population hits 36,000
in dramatic

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.200
rise
But this is not the end of the story!

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.899
Think about this: here at
VisualPolitik, we always say that the world

00:01:56.899 --> 00:01:57.899
is getting better.

00:01:57.899 --> 00:02:01.000
We are wealthier, healthier and have better
lifestyles.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.890
This
is, partly, because… stuff is getting cheaper!

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.390
Here’s an
example: during the 70s, only 20% of Americans

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:09.280
had ever
flown on a plane.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.280
Why?

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:15.830
Because a Los Angeles-New York
trip cost 1500 of today’s dollars.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:19.030
Now, this very same flight
is just 200 USD.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:22.810
Thanks to this, I’m sure all of you have
already travelled more than your grandparents

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.170
did in their
entire Lifetimes.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.950
The same goes for other goods.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.720
Everything
is becoming more and more affordable… except

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.220
houses!

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.160
No matter if you rent or buy: you have to
make the same, if

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.160
not more, sacrifices than your grandparents’
generation.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.390
Today, in 2019, there are people in Stockholm
living in 150

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:44.390
square meter flats.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.670
In fact, it’s not uncommon to meet
couples in Sweden who want to divorce but

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.240
are waiting for
their spouse to find another flat.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.240
Do you call that welfare?

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.550
But the real question here is…

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.550
Why are flat rentals going so
sky high?

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.620
Can we solve this problem with better policies?

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.141
Today we are going to answer these questions
but, before

00:02:59.141 --> 00:03:03.390
we do, let’s take a look back at history.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.430
SUPPLY, DEMAND AND…

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.000
THE REST
It is a universal and logical fact: the population

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.470
grows in
every country.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.940
Just a decade ago, America had 300 million
people.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.900
Now they have 27 million more.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.450
But not all cities
grow equally.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.490
If you’re watching this video from Detroit
or

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.940
Cleveland, you’ve probably noticed that
there are less and

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.160
less people.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.260
Most of your friends are leaving the city.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.940
And
where are they going?

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:33.940
Exactly!

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.930
To more prosperous cities
like L.A. or New York.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.340
So what does this mean?

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.090
It means
that the demand for living in those cities

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.400
keeps growing
higher.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.930
This is totally OK and it’s natural.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:44.420
But here is the real problem!

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.060
If we’re talking about a normal
market, supply can rise to satisfy the demand.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.420
For example,
let’s look at oil markets.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.100
We told you a million times here at
VisualPolitik, how Saudi Arabia decreases

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:59.040
or increases its
oil barrel production depending on the market’s

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.040
needs.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.040
It’s
easy.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.881
They can increase production from one month
to the

00:04:02.881 --> 00:04:03.881
other.

00:04:03.881 --> 00:04:05.270
But when it comes to real estate, increasing
supply

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:08.240
means building more houses… and this takes
time.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.599
Besides, the investment for a new block of
flats is huge and

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:13.990
the profit margins are around 3%.

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:18.910
And the truth is that we
need many, many houses to cover demand.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:24.360
California would need to build 3.5 million
housing units by

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.900
2025 to close its housing gap –Extract from
a report from

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.540
McKinsey Global Institute, October 2016
But why do developers have so many problems

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:33.790
building
homes?

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:34.790
We are talking economics.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.500
With so much demand,
you could make a great business with housing

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.360
in California,
right?

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.760
Well… of course you can.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.510
There’s just one problem!

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.510
Zoning laws.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.460
You see, the real estate market is actually
tightly regulated by city halls.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.680
If you want to build in L.A.,
you need a permit that can take more than

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:50.750
3 years to get.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:52.930
And all of this happens because city corporations
are the

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.930
ones who choose where and how to build.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.370
So how does the
City hall know how many dwellings they need?

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.440
Mainly, they
look at population growth.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.200
If you check the data, you’ll see
that the housing census in L.A. has mirrored

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.460
its
demographic growth.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.330
And although it makes sense on the
surface, this is a huge mistake.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:13.780
Because population is one
thing and demand is another.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:18.770
For example, imagine a
couple who lives in one house.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:19.940
If they get divorced, all of a

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.380
sudden, they need an extra home.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:22.560
So, there you go!

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.530
With
the same population, we just created twice

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:25.530
as much
demand.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.070
And the reality is that we have a higher and
higher

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:30.190
single and divorced population.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:34.600
This is not happening just in
L.A. but also in Madrid, Berlin and almost

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:35.600
any other city.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.790
Many experts say that the solution is to build
more houses.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.230
Others advocate for abolishing zoning laws.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.360
But, in Europe,
many cities bet on price controls.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.710
Do you think these
policies are working?

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.140
Is this a good or a bad idea?

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:52.420
Let’s
have a look, shall we?

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:58.560
THE OTHER STOCKHOLM SYNDROME
Usually, when we talk about Sweden, it’s

00:05:58.560 --> 00:05:59.919
because we’re
using them as a model.

00:05:59.919 --> 00:06:03.290
Swedish people enjoy a large
welfare state alongside a dynamic economy

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:04.760
with innovative
companies.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:08.139
Among those, there’s Europe’s most famous
startup… even though it might not stay in

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:10.030
Sweden for much
longer.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:11.520
Check this out!

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.460
Spotify threats to leave Sweden spur startup
protest in

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.460
Stockholm
So why do startups want to leave Sweden?

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:18.460
Is it because of
the high taxes?

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:19.460
Not really!

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:20.460
The main reason is that they
can’t hire foreign talent.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:21.460
And you might be saying…

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:22.460
Come
on, Simon!

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:23.460
Everybody knows that Sweden is very open to
immigrants!

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:24.460
And yes, they are!

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:25.990
But good luck moving to
Stockholm and finding a flat!

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:29.060
While we were making this
video, we spoke to a man who worked for a

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.830
Swedish
startup for a year and a half.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:34.820
He made a salary of 2900
euros a month, but more than half of that

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:38.220
went toward
paying the rent on a 500 square foot flat.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.350
And believe me,
this was not the worst case.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:44.889
Finding a flat in Stockholm is
so hard that some people spend months in an

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:45.889
AirBNB.

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:50.320
And
you might wonder… why?

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.840
It all started in the 50s.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:55.020
Sweden
needed houses.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:58.900
So the social democratic government
created the Million Houses Policy.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:02.419
The idea was to build
public houses for rent at very cheap prices.

00:07:02.419 --> 00:07:04.699
During those
years, every Swedish person could apply for

00:07:04.699 --> 00:07:08.090
a public house
and pay rent at prices equivalent to 400 or

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:09.410
300 euros today.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:14.740
But things changed in the 90s.

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:18.300
During those years, Sweden
suffered one of the worst debt crises in their

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.300
history.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:20.660
No
money meant no more houses.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.680
But the rent regulations
remained.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.199
So you either went for a public house or you
had

00:07:25.199 --> 00:07:28.610
to find a place in an extremely scarce rental
market.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:31.230
Basically, in Sweden they have the so-called
first hand

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:32.230
market.

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:33.669
Those are the public houses.

00:07:33.669 --> 00:07:39.509
If you’re Swedish, the first thing you do
when you turn 18 is

00:07:39.509 --> 00:07:40.509
apply there.

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:43.840
If you’re lucky, in 13 or so years, you’ll
get a 2

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:47.180
bedroom apartment for just 600 euros a month.

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.560
In other
words, what you don’t pay with money, you

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.710
pay with time.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:56.930
This explains why 25% of Swedes live with
their parents

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.250
until they turn 30.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:02.060
And also, it explains why this society is
turning from renting

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:03.060
to owning.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.130
With low rates for mortgages, most Swedes
buy

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:06.360
as soon as they can.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.560
But the real question is… what happens with
the people

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:09.699
who aren’t born in Sweden?

00:08:09.699 --> 00:08:12.889
What happens if you just
moved there and have no family to stay with?

00:08:12.889 --> 00:08:15.880
Well… in that
case… welcome to the wonderful world of

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.490
the second hand
market!

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:19.461
Basically, Sweden believes that you shouldn’t
make

00:08:19.461 --> 00:08:21.700
money from renting.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:25.900
The law has lots of ways to ban people
from renting.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:27.650
And long term rentals are forbidden.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:28.801
But even
if you are allowed to have a tenant for a

00:08:28.801 --> 00:08:31.920
one year contract,
the community of the building can deny you

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:32.920
permission.

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:33.920
Yes, you heard that right.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.400
Your neighbours can say that you
can’t rent out your place or that you have

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:37.599
to kick out your
tenant.

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:39.691
This explains why, with so many restrictions,
there

00:08:39.691 --> 00:08:41.589
are almost no available flats.

00:08:41.589 --> 00:08:45.339
So, despite the great welfare
system, many people live in 200 square meter

00:08:45.339 --> 00:08:46.339
flats.

00:08:46.339 --> 00:08:49.230
But,
again, the worst problem is not the inconvenience.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.509
It’s this.

00:08:51.509 --> 00:08:55.689
Sweden probes riot in mainly immigrant Stockholm
suburb

00:08:55.689 --> 00:08:56.689
Exactly!

00:08:56.689 --> 00:08:58.321
The poor immigrants who have no startup to
find a

00:08:58.321 --> 00:09:00.809
flat for them have to go to the worst neighbourhoods.

00:09:00.809 --> 00:09:02.809
And
this is how they form ghettos.

00:09:02.809 --> 00:09:06.730
And… yes, OK, I know that all
of you have seen those conspiracy videos where

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:09.309
they
exaggerate the gang violence in Sweden.

00:09:09.309 --> 00:09:11.420
But let’s be
honest, even though the problem is not as

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:15.170
big as some
media portrayals, Sweden has a clear problem

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:16.309
with
criminality.

00:09:16.309 --> 00:09:18.399
But we’ll talk about this in a future video.

00:09:18.399 --> 00:09:19.399
In the
meantime, by the way, if you’re new to this

00:09:19.399 --> 00:09:20.889
channel and
you’re enjoying this video so far, remember

00:09:20.889 --> 00:09:22.660
that we publish
brand new videos every week.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.920
So subscribe to VisualPolitik
and hit that bell button so you receive a

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.949
notification on your
phone anytime we post.

00:09:28.949 --> 00:09:31.100
But let’s get back to our story.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.360
Of course, this Stockholm
crisis could be solved by building more houses.

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.269
But, even if
you built 80,000 new dwellings a year as many

00:09:38.269 --> 00:09:41.269
have
suggested, you’d also badly need to change

00:09:41.269 --> 00:09:43.309
those renting
laws.

00:09:43.309 --> 00:09:45.499
But now you might wonder…

00:09:45.499 --> 00:09:47.619
OK, Simon… give us a
solution!

00:09:47.619 --> 00:09:50.309
Is there any example of a city that solved
this

00:09:50.309 --> 00:09:51.429
renting problem?

00:09:51.429 --> 00:09:53.180
And the answer is yes.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:57.990
Let’s check it out,
shall we?

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.949
VIENNA AND RENTS-FOR-ALL SYSTEM
Austria is one of Europe’s wealthiest countries.

00:10:02.949 --> 00:10:05.990
The
average salary here is over 2,700 euros a

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:06.990
month.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.670
And, if
you live in Vienna, it’s not hard to find

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.569
jobs paying more
than 3500 euros a month.

00:10:11.569 --> 00:10:16.809
Nevertheless, rent costs are the
same as a way poorer city like Madrid, Spain.

00:10:16.809 --> 00:10:20.230
For 1500
euros, you can find an amazing 860 square

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:22.000
foot flat in the
center of the city.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.209
And I know what you’re thinking…

00:10:23.209 --> 00:10:25.439
Now the guys at
VisualPolitik are going to tell me another

00:10:25.439 --> 00:10:28.999
story about how a
city embraced the free market with no remorse

00:10:28.999 --> 00:10:31.759
and lived
happily ever after, right?

00:10:31.759 --> 00:10:33.079
Well… nope.

00:10:33.079 --> 00:10:34.329
The opposite is true.

00:10:34.329 --> 00:10:36.699
Basically, the Vienna council is something
like a developer

00:10:36.699 --> 00:10:38.689
and a rental agency at the same time.

00:10:38.689 --> 00:10:42.149
They are the city’s
biggest landlord.

00:10:42.149 --> 00:10:48.850
You see, after World War II, half of Vienna
ended up completely devastated by the bombings.

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:51.819
So the
city hall paid for all of the reconstruction.

00:10:51.819 --> 00:10:54.110
That’s when they
said ‘Wait a minute!

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:55.369
Since we paid for all of this… aren’t
we

00:10:55.369 --> 00:10:58.069
entitled to rent it out under our own conditions?’

00:10:58.069 --> 00:11:01.500
This is how
the World’s most ambitious public program

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:02.500
started.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:05.060
Here’s how it works: 80% of Viennese people
live in rental

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.060
flats.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.019
60% of those are public housing.

00:11:08.019 --> 00:11:10.100
Some of the
buildings are from cooperatives and others

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:11.309
belong to the
city hall.

00:11:11.309 --> 00:11:13.860
But wait a minute because we are not talking
about normal

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:14.860
rental agreements!

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:18.329
In fact, we could call them something
just short of limited property.

00:11:18.329 --> 00:11:22.269
Basically, rent contracts in
Vienna last forever… even after you’re

00:11:22.269 --> 00:11:23.269
dead!

00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:24.809
Yes, rental
contracts can be inherited as well.

00:11:24.809 --> 00:11:28.879
So, as long as you don’t
move, you can say the flat is yours.

00:11:28.879 --> 00:11:29.879
And oh boy!

00:11:29.879 --> 00:11:31.459
You gotta
see those houses.

00:11:31.459 --> 00:11:35.119
In America and the UK, we have this
notion that public housing is for the poor

00:11:35.119 --> 00:11:36.410
and it’s in bad
neighbourhoods.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:41.329
But here, you can find public houses even
in the best neighbourhoods and they are amazing:

00:11:41.329 --> 00:11:47.809
swimming pools, gyms, even saunas.

00:11:47.809 --> 00:11:49.639
In some cases, you
can even find pretty absurd situations.

00:11:49.639 --> 00:11:51.800
While we were doing the research for this
video, we spoke

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.619
to Vienna’s city hall and they gave us lots
of cases.

00:11:54.619 --> 00:11:55.959
For
example, there’s a young woman who lives

00:11:55.959 --> 00:11:59.259
in an 860
square foot apartment in the center of the

00:11:59.259 --> 00:12:00.259
city.

00:12:00.259 --> 00:12:01.259
How much
do you think she pays?

00:12:01.259 --> 00:12:02.259
400 euros!

00:12:02.259 --> 00:12:04.230
That’s less than 500
dollars!

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:07.269
But her neighbour, living in a similar flat,
pays 2000

00:12:07.269 --> 00:12:08.269
euros.

00:12:08.269 --> 00:12:09.269
Why?

00:12:09.269 --> 00:12:10.920
He lives in one of the apartments that aren’t
public!

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:14.019
So just like in Stockholm, Vienna also has
two types

00:12:14.019 --> 00:12:16.860
of renting: controlled prices and free prices.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:18.560
And you might
be wondering…

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:21.740
How come they don’t have waiting lists
here?

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:26.379
Well, here’s your answer!

00:12:26.379 --> 00:12:30.699
Annually, Vienna spends about 600 million
euros ($682

00:12:30.699 --> 00:12:33.220
million) on social housing.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:37.949
In comparison, Germany — with
a population over 45 times Vienna&amp;#39;s — spends

00:12:37.949 --> 00:12:40.360
about 400
million euros nationwide.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:43.160
And now you might wonder… how does this
system work?

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.540
Let’s say that you move to Vienna.

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.959
In the first few years,
you can’t apply for public housing.

00:12:46.959 --> 00:12:47.959
But no problem!

00:12:47.959 --> 00:12:50.600
There
are more than 290,000 flats on the free market.

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:53.540
So it’s not
hard to find a place and the prices aren’t

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:54.959
crazy because
there’s a large supply.

00:12:54.959 --> 00:12:58.829
Then, after only 2 or 3 years, you
can apply for a public house.

00:12:58.829 --> 00:13:02.089
Depending on your family
status, your income and your pickiness, it

00:13:02.089 --> 00:13:04.259
might be easier or
harder for you to find a home.

00:13:04.259 --> 00:13:06.380
But, usually, we’re talking about waiting
times of no more

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:07.380
than 2 years.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:11.199
Of course, as we always say on VisualPolitik,
not everything is sunshine and rainbows.

00:13:11.199 --> 00:13:13.279
The first issue
with this system is that you don’t wanna

00:13:13.279 --> 00:13:15.369
move out of your
flat once you find it.

00:13:15.369 --> 00:13:17.519
This model pretty much reduces your
freedom to choose.

00:13:17.519 --> 00:13:20.989
Basically, it’s not you who gets to
choose but the city hall.

00:13:20.989 --> 00:13:23.970
And remember that your house
determines many things about your lifestyle.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:30.790
But there is another issue here: the sustainability.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.410
This
system relies heavily on just one actor: the

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:34.579
municipal
corporation.

00:13:34.579 --> 00:13:38.579
This means that, as long as the local
government has the money, there’s no problem.

00:13:38.579 --> 00:13:41.120
But they
just need one crisis and there will be no

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:42.120
more houses.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:43.679
And,
since there aren’t many private developers

00:13:43.679 --> 00:13:46.369
because there
are no incentives, they could easily end up

00:13:46.369 --> 00:13:48.619
with a housing
shortage situation.

00:13:48.619 --> 00:13:50.989
And this would lead to a lot of problems.

00:13:50.989 --> 00:13:57.160
But we have to admit that, so far, this model
seems to be

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:58.399
working.

00:13:58.399 --> 00:13:59.480
So the question now is…

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:03.920
Would it be possible to combine
this Viennese model with a more free market

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:04.920
solution?

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:06.209
Do
you think this system could be applied in

00:14:06.209 --> 00:14:07.209
other cities?

00:14:07.209 --> 00:14:10.269
Would you mind renting the same flat for your
entire life if

00:14:10.269 --> 00:14:11.509
the conditions were good enough?

00:14:11.509 --> 00:14:13.970
Please, leave your answer in the comment section
below.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:15.980
Before finishing, let me give a special thanks
to MARC

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:18.720
SOLO and JAIME CHAPINAL for helping us with
the facts

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:19.720
on Stockholm.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:22.379
Also, let me thank Vienna’s City hall for
answering all of our questions.

00:14:22.379 --> 00:14:23.600
They were extremely nice.

